# Clean Code (guidance, not gates)

Advisory craft guidance — nothing here is CI-enforced (that's `file-structure.mdc`). Exercise judgment;
when a principle conflicts with readability, readability wins. Full version: `CODE_STYLE.md`.

Optimize for the next person reading the code. Clarity beats cleverness.

## Functions
- One job; the name says what it does (if it needs "and", it's two functions).
- Short enough to see at once (~50 lines is a gut check, not a limit).
- Few parameters (>3–4 → group into a small object / dataclass / model).
- Return early with guard clauses; avoid deep nesting.
- Prefer pure functions — push I/O (DB, network, storage) to the edges. Backend: rules in `service.py`,
  I/O in routers / `shared/storage.py`.
- No hidden side effects or global-state reach-ins.

## Naming
- Name by intent, not type/mechanism (`activeStudents`, not `list2`).
- Booleans read as questions: `isVerified`, `hasJoined`.
- Avoid abbreviations; consistency over brevity.

## Abstraction & complexity
- DRY, but not prematurely — rule of three. A wrong abstraction costs more than a little duplication.
- Shallow nesting matters more than line count. One level of abstraction per function.
- Prefer types/enums to make illegal states unrepresentable.

## Comments & errors
- Comments explain **why**, not what. Delete dead code and stale comments.
- Fail loudly and early; handle errors at the layer that can act on them.

## Language notes
- Python: type hints on public functions; small models over loose dicts.
- Dart/Flutter: `const` where possible; extract widgets instead of growing `build`; state in providers,
  presentation in widgets.

The test: *could a teammate understand this without asking you?* If not, fix the clarity — not the line count.
